\title{\code{\verb~|simd::extract~} with a computed index does not compile for a 32-bit target}
\taxon{Problem}
\date{2026-11-16}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{schrodingerzy}
\tag{compiler}
\tag{simd}
\meta{status}{closed}
\meta{completed}{2026-11-16}
\meta{created}{2026-11-16}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\strong{Status:} closed by \pr{1040}.}

\p{\code{\verb~|core::simd::extract~} is documented to take a computed index into the last dimension. For \code{\verb~|wasm32-wasip1~} such a call fails in the last step of code generation. Found while trying a vector of four 64-bit words as the representation of \code{\verb~|std::regex::alphabet::bits::Bits~} for \pr{1039}: the standard library then no longer builds for WebAssembly.}

\p{\strong{Demonstration.}}

\pre{\verb~|
import core::simd;

pub fn lane(v: Simd<[u64; 4]>, index: u64) -> u64 {
    simd::extract(v, index)
}
~}

\pre{\verb~|
rrc lane.rr --package-name repro --extern core=core.rri --target-triple wasm32-wasip1 --emit llvm-ir -o lane.ll
error: LLVM Translation failed for operation: builtin.unrealized_conversion_cast
~}

\p{The same file compiles for \code{\verb~|x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu~}. An index that is a literal compiles for both. With \code{\verb~|--emit mlir-llvm~} the function is left as:}

\pre{\verb~|
%0 = llvm.trunc %arg1 : i64 to i32
%1 = builtin.unrealized_conversion_cast %0 : i32 to index
%2 = builtin.unrealized_conversion_cast %1 : index to i64
%3 = llvm.extractelement %arg0[%2 : i64] : vector<4xi64>
~}

\p{\strong{Cause.} Two conversions disagree on the width of \code{\verb~|index~}. Reussir's own lowering takes it from the data layout, 32 bits where pointers are 32 bits (\code{\verb~|buildLowerOptions~} in \code{\verb~|lib/Conversion/TypeConverter/TypeConverter.cpp~}), so \code{\verb~|arith.index_castui~} becomes a truncation to \code{\verb~|i32~}. The upstream \code{\verb~|convert-vector-to-llvm~} pass is created with its default options in \code{\verb~|reussirCreateConvertVectorToLLVMPass~} (\code{\verb~|lib/CAPI/Passes.cpp~}), builds its own type converter with a 64-bit \code{\verb~|index~}, and lowers the position of \code{\verb~|vector.extract~} to \code{\verb~|i64~}. The two casts between them do not cancel.}

\p{\strong{Upstream.} From llvm/llvm-project#206380 on, \code{\verb~|convert-vector-to-llvm~} builds its options from the data layout of the module. The pinned MLIR 23.1.3 does not have that change, and the data layout Reussir attaches carries no entry for \code{\verb~|index~} yet.}

\p{\strong{Fix, \pr{1040}.} \code{\verb~|reussir-lowering-basic-ops~} runs right before the upstream pass and holds the type converter of the target. Where \code{\verb~|index~} is not 64 bits wide it converts \code{\verb~|vector.extract~} and \code{\verb~|vector.insert~} at a computed position with the upstream patterns under that converter; every other operation is left to the upstream pass, and a 64-bit target is not touched. Among the SIMD tests only computed positions were affected. \code{\verb~|simd_construct.rr~} is now also compiled for \code{\verb~|wasm32-unknown-unknown~}.}
